{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_rwxf3wkdjx2kjpnm35y7dj5wekzn2io7c7brdo5ivvaceqh5g24a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. All four phases pass, and I verified every artifact on disk myself.\n\n## What the library is\n\nA single-function C library: `void update_frame_header(tflac *t)` (`c_src/src/lib.c`, 145 lines) that packs a FLAC frame header into `t->frame_header` from four `switch`es on `cur_blocksize`, `samplerate`, `channel_mode`, and `bitdepth`.\n\n## Phase A — artifacts (in `translation/`)\n\n| file | content |\n|---|---|\n| `SYMBOLS.md` | 1 exported symbol (`update_frame_header`) in both `.so`s; **symbol diff empty**; 0 undefined non-libc symbols in Rust; verified struct ABI (size 24, align 4, offsets 0/4/8/12/16/20) with `offsetof` on the C side |\n| `ERRORS.md` | **21 rows.** A mechanical grep proved the C has *zero* `return`/`assert`/`NULL`-check/error-code constructs, so the error surface is entirely silent-fallthrough (`default:` arms, guarded inner `if`s), one unsigned-underflow path, and one UB path |\n| `CONFIGS.md` | **33 rows** — cross-product of 15 blocksize classes × 17 samplerate classes × 4 channel modes × 5 channel-count classes × 7 bitdepth classes, pruned to what the C distinguishes |\n\n## Phases B/C/D — results\n\n- **`cargo check`**: clean from the start, 0 errors, 0 warnings.\n- **Phase B** (33 tests): all `CONFIGS.md` rows pass, ~30 M byte-for-byte comparisons per run (fixed-seed SplitMix64 fuzz + exhaustive per-axis sweeps + a 538 200-case complete class cross-product).\n- **Phase C** (23 tests): all 21 `ERRORS.md` rows pass, plus all 256 out-of-range `channel_mode` enum values, null pointers, zero/oversized lengths, and off-by-one boundaries.\n- **Phase D**: `nm -D` diff empty for **both** debug and release cdylibs; suite green across all 3 feature configurations × 2 build profiles (6 full runs, `./verify.sh`).\n\nTests never call Rust directly — both `.so`s are loaded via `libloading` and compared through a guarded 56-byte buffer, so padding bytes and out-of-bounds writes are also checked.\n\n## Two findings worth flagging\n\n**1. No translation bugs found.** `src/lib.rs` is byte-identical to what I started with — the translation was already faithful, including the subtle bits (`channels.wrapping_sub(1) << 4` matching C's unsigned underflow, `% 4` mode folding, and the `default:` arm that is provably unreachable).\n\nSince \"everything passed\" is weak evidence on its own, I **mutation-tested the harness**: 11 deliberate bugs injected into the Rust one at a time. 10 were caught. The 11th (`cur_blocksize <= 256` → `< 256`) is a *provably equivalent* mutant, not a coverage gap — the ternary is only reached when no `case` matched, and `256` is itself a case label (`lib.c:29`), so the predicates can never disagree on a reachable value.\n\n**2. One real behavioural difference, confined to debug builds.** On `update_frame_header(NULL)`:\n\n| library | signal |\n|---|---|\n| C | `SIGSEGV` (139) |\n| Rust `--release` | `SIGSEGV` (139) — identical |\n| Rust debug | `SIGABRT` (134) |\n\nrustc injects a `debug_assertions`-only null-pointer UB check that panics (then aborts, since it can't unwind out of `extern \"C\"`) *before* the faulting store. It's absent from the release cdylib, which is what an external caller loads. I did **not** paper over this: `err_01` asserts C dies of `SIGSEGV`, requires the release `.so` to match exactly, and only tolerates a differing signal if it can prove the `.so` is a debug build (by grepping it for rustc's `null pointer dereference occurred` string) *and* the signal is exactly `SIGABRT`. Documented in `ERRORS.md`.\n\nReproduce everything with `cd translation && ./verify.sh` (~30 s)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":605,"cache_read_input_tokens":142919,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_