 unsigned underflow, `% 4` mode folding, and the `default:` arm that is provably unreachable).\n\nSince \"everything passed\" is weak evidence on its own, I **mutation-tested the harness**: 11 deliberate bugs injected into the Rust one at a time. 10 were caught. The 11th (`cur_blocksize <= 256` → `< 256`) is a *provably equivalent* mutant, not a coverage gap — the ternary is only reached when no `case` matched, and `256` is itself a case label (`lib.c:29`), so the predicates can never disagree on a reachable value.\n\n**2. One real behavioural difference, confined to debug builds.** On `update_frame_header(NULL)`:\n\n| library | signal |\n|---|---|\n| C | `SIGSEGV` (139) |\n| Rust `--release` | `SIGSEGV` (139) — identical |\n| Rust debug | `SIGABRT` (134) |\n\nrustc injects a `debug_assertions`-only null-pointer UB check that panics (then aborts, since it can't unwind out of `extern \"C\"`) *before* the faulting store. It's absent from the release cdylib, which is what an external caller loads. I did **not** paper over this: `err_01` asserts C dies of `SIGSEGV`, requires the release `.so` to match exactly, and only tolerates a differing signal if it can prove the `.so` is a debug build (by grepping it for rustc's `null pointer dereference occurred` string) *and* the signal is exactly `SIGABRT`. Documented in `ERRORS.md`.\n\nReproduce everything with `cd translation && ./verify.sh` (~30 s).","ttft_ms":4539,"ttft_stream_ms":4352,"time_to_request_ms":159,"type":"result","duration_ms":1475643,"uuid":"dd773509-33f3-4470-a2bd-b8ed88605dd6","queued_turn_count":0}
